<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uganda People's Defence Force</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-defence-force">People's Defence Force</a> is the armed forces of Uganda, formed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-resistance-army">National Resistance Army</a> and given its present name under the constitution of 1995. It fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-resistance-army">Lord's Resistance Army</a> in the north for two decades, intervened in the Congo wars between 1996 and 2003, and since 2007 has provided the largest contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-defence-force.jpg" alt="Uganda People's Defence Force" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skjoldbro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Uganda_People%27s_Defence_Force_(2019).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of Uganda's armed forces go back to 1902, when the Uganda Battalion of the King's African Rifles was formed, and Ugandan soldiers served in that regiment in both world wars. As independence approached the army was used more often against unrest at home: in January 1960 troops sent to Bugisu and Bukedi districts killed 12 people, injured several hundred and arrested more than 1,000, and in March 1962 control of the military passed to the Ministry of Home Affairs.</p><p>At independence on 9 October 1962 the 4th Battalion of the King's African Rifles, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, became the Uganda Rifles, while British officers kept most high commands. Ugandan soldiers complained that this blocked their promotion and kept their pay low. Each regime after independence enlarged the army, usually by recruiting from one region or ethnic group, and each used it to put down political unrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In January 1971 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> and his followers in the army seized power in a coup d'état, and in 1972 Amin's Malire Mechanised Regiment expelled a small force that had crossed from Tanzania. The Uganda Army Air Force, founded in 1964 with Israeli aid and later supplied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, was effectively wiped out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-tanzania-war">Uganda–Tanzania War</a> of 1978–1979. After that war the new government had fewer than 1,000 troops, organised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-national-liberation-army">Uganda National Liberation Army</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-resistance-army">National Resistance Army</a>, which had won its war and whose senior officers held key political posts, was cut under pressure from donors from 100,000 to 40,000 men between 1990 and 1996. Yet the defence budget rose from $44 million in 1991 to $200 million in 2004, which Somerville ascribes to the war in the north, the intervention in the Congo and corruption, including salaries drawn for ghost soldiers who did not exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>From 2007 to 2011 the International Institute for Strategic Studies estimated the force at 40,000–45,000, in land forces and an air wing, with recruits taken in each year. More than 6,200 Ugandan soldiers served with the African Union Mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, whose commander in 2009, Major General Nathan Mugisha, was wounded in a car bomb attack on 17 September 2009 that killed nine soldiers. From 2014 Uganda also provided the guard unit of the United Nations Assistance Mission in Somalia at Mogadishu Airport, and in July 2016 its troops evacuated up to 40,000 Ugandans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uganda People's Defence Force” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
